+# Best AI Tools for Lawyers in 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-lawyers-2026?style=social)
+
+> Best AI tools for lawyers in 2026 are increasingly open source, self-hosted, and GitHub-first. This curated awesome list focuses only on public GitHub repositories that help lawyers, legal ops teams, and researchers with contract analysis, OCR, redaction, transcription, legal search, local LLM workflows, and document-heavy knowledge work.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `OpenContracts`, `LexNLP`, `LawGlance`, and `OpenNyAI` are the strongest clearly legal-oriented open source repos in this niche, but the category is still thin.
+- `PaddleOCR`, `Docling`, `Marker`, `Surya`, and `Unstructured` are the most useful document-intelligence building blocks for contract review and legal ingestion pipelines.
+- `Presidio`, `Phileas`, `Philter`, `scrubadub`, and `pii-redaction` are the most relevant open source privacy layers for de-identification and redaction workflows.
+- `Whisper`, `faster-whisper`, `WhisperX`, `pyannote-audio`, and `Vosk` are the best open source choices for deposition, interview, and meeting transcription stacks.
+- `Haystack`, `LlamaIndex`, `Qdrant`, `Open WebUI`, `AnythingLLM`, and `Ollama` are strong foundations for self-hosted legal research assistants and private RAG systems.
+
+## Why This List
+Law firms, in-house legal teams, and solo practitioners increasingly want AI tooling they can inspect, self-host, and adapt to privileged or confidential workflows. That makes open source especially important for the best AI tools for lawyers in 2026: it improves auditability, privacy, deployment flexibility, and long-term control over document pipelines that would otherwise be locked into proprietary SaaS.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Legal AI And Contract Intelligence
+
+Legal-specific open source GitHub repositories are still a real gap. This category includes only four well-scoped public repos that are directly legal-focused, which is below the ideal five-tool threshold.
+
+#### [OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts)
+> **Description:** OpenContracts describes itself as a self-hosted platform where teams build knowledge bases from documents and let AI agents work alongside humans to search, annotate, extract, and reason over that material. Its README emphasizes corpus versioning, PDF-native annotation, vector search, threaded collaboration, extraction workflows, and MCP support. For legal teams, that makes it one of the rare open source platforms built explicitly around contracts and governed text rather than generic chat-over-PDF. Verified from GitHub: last commit `2026-03-20`, about `1.3k` stars, primary language `Python`, license `AGPL-3.0`.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts)
+- **Stars:** 1.3k ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** AGPL-3.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-20
+- **Category:** legal-ai, contract-analysis, knowledge-base
+- **Best for:** self-hosted contract repositories and AI-assisted legal annotation
+
+---
+
+#### [LawGlance](https://github.com/lawglance/lawglance)
+> **Description:** LawGlance presents itself as a free, open source RAG-based AI legal assistant built to make legal guidance more accessible. The README highlights coverage of Indian legal sources, a Streamlit interface, Redis-backed chat history, and an architecture aimed at fast legal question answering over curated laws and statutes. It is not a polished enterprise platform, but it is a relevant open repo because it demonstrates how a public legal assistant can be assembled with retrieval, prompts, and searchable source material. Verified from GitHub: last commit `2025-12-30`, about `239` stars, primary language `Python`, license `Apache-2.0`.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/lawglance/lawglance](https://github.com/lawglance/lawglance)
+- **Stars:** 239 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-12-30
+- **Category:** legal-assistant, rag, legal-research
+- **Best for:** open source legal Q&A assistants over statute collections
+
+---
+
+#### [LexNLP](https://github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp)
+> **Description:** LexNLP is one of the longest-running open source libraries for legal text extraction. Its README focuses on working with real, unstructured legal text such as contracts, policies, plans, and procedures; extracting monetary amounts, dates, citations, constraints, courts, and clause-level signals; and providing tokenization and classification tailored to legal language. That makes it more of a legal NLP toolkit than a finished app, but it remains highly relevant for lawyers building custom clause analysis or structured review workflows. Verified from GitHub: last update `2024-05-27`, about `753` stars, primary language `Jupyter Notebook`, license `AGPL-3.0`.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp](https://github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp)
+- **Stars:** 753 ⭐
+- **Language:** Jupyter Notebook
+- **License:** AGPL-3.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2024-05-27
+- **Category:** legal-nlp, extraction, clause-analysis
+- **Best for:** custom legal text extraction and contract clause pipelines
+
+---
+
+#### [Opennyai](https://github.com/OpenNyAI/Opennyai)
+> **Description:** Opennyai is a Python NLP pipeline for Indian legal documents, especially court judgments. The README highlights unified access to three pretrained models: named entity recognition, rhetorical role classification, and extractive summarization. Unlike generic NLP repos, it is focused on judgment text preprocessing and running pretrained legal models on custom inputs, which makes it useful for legal research teams working with case law corpora. It is narrower in scope than a full legal workspace, but it is a genuine legal-tech building block hosted publicly on GitHub. Verified from GitHub: last visible release `2024-05-08`, about `87` stars, primary language `Python`, license `MIT`.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/OpenNyAI/Opennyai](https://github.com/OpenNyAI/Opennyai)
+- **Stars:** 87 ⭐
