+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for lawyers in 2026?
+For legal-specific repos, start with `OpenContracts`, `LawGlance`, `LexNLP`, and `Opennyai`. For real-world legal operations, the strongest stack usually combines those with document tooling like `PaddleOCR` or `Docling`, privacy tooling like `Presidio`, and a self-hosted RAG layer like `LlamaIndex`, `Haystack`, `Qdrant`, `AnythingLLM`, or `Open WebUI`.
+
+### Are there many open source legal AI repositories on GitHub?
+No. That is the biggest finding from this research. There are plenty of open source AI components useful to lawyers, but truly lawyer-specific GitHub repositories are still limited. This README documents that gap instead of pretending there are dozens of mature open source legal platforms already available.
+
+### Which open source AI tools are best for contract review and clause extraction?
+`OpenContracts` and `LexNLP` are the most directly relevant legal-specific picks. For upstream parsing and OCR, `Docling`, `Marker`, `Surya`, `PaddleOCR`, and `OCRmyPDF` are strong complements.
+
+### Which GitHub repositories help lawyers build private RAG or document chat systems?
+`Haystack`, `LlamaIndex`, `Qdrant`, `AnythingLLM`, `Open WebUI`, and `Ollama` are the best starting points on this list for self-hosted legal research assistants over private files.
+
+### What open source tools help redact privileged or sensitive legal data?
+`Presidio` is the strongest general-purpose choice. `Phileas`, `Philter`, `scrubadub`, and `pii-redaction` are also useful depending on whether you need a library, policy engine, or API service.
+
+### Which open source AI repos are best for deposition transcription?
+`Whisper`, `faster-whisper`, and `WhisperX` are the core picks. Add `pyannote-audio` when speaker diarization matters, and use `Vosk` when offline deployment is more important than having the newest model family.
+
