+## TL;DR
+- `OpenContracts`, `LexNLP`, `LawGlance`, and `OpenNyAI` are the strongest clearly legal-oriented open source repos in this niche, but the category is still thin.
+- `PaddleOCR`, `Docling`, `Marker`, `Surya`, and `Unstructured` are the most useful document-intelligence building blocks for contract review and legal ingestion pipelines.
+- `Presidio`, `Phileas`, `Philter`, `scrubadub`, and `pii-redaction` are the most relevant open source privacy layers for de-identification and redaction workflows.
+- `Whisper`, `faster-whisper`, `WhisperX`, `pyannote-audio`, and `Vosk` are the best open source choices for deposition, interview, and meeting transcription stacks.
+- `Haystack`, `LlamaIndex`, `Qdrant`, `Open WebUI`, `AnythingLLM`, and `Ollama` are strong foundations for self-hosted legal research assistants and private RAG systems.
+
+## Why This List
+Law firms, in-house legal teams, and solo practitioners increasingly want AI tooling they can inspect, self-host, and adapt to privileged or confidential workflows. That makes open source especially important for the best AI tools for lawyers in 2026: it improves auditability, privacy, deployment flexibility, and long-term control over document pipelines that would otherwise be locked into proprietary SaaS.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Legal AI And Contract Intelligence
+
+Legal-specific open source GitHub repositories are still a real gap. This category includes only four well-scoped public repos that are directly legal-focused, which is below the ideal five-tool threshold.
+
+#### [OpenContracts](https://github.com/Open-Source-Legal/OpenContracts)
+> **Description:** OpenContracts describes itself as a self-hosted platform where teams build knowledge bases from documents and let AI agents work alongside humans to search, annotate, extract, and reason over that material. Its README emphasizes corpus versioning, PDF-native annotation, vector search, threaded collaboration, extraction workflows, and MCP support. For legal teams, that makes it one of the rare open source platforms built explicitly around contracts and governed text rather than generic chat-over-PDF. Verified from GitHub: last commit `2026-03-20`, about `1.3k` stars, primary language `Python`, license `AGPL-3.0`.
